= Category — Technical =

== Realist / conceptualist approaches ==
A realist category scheme attempts to reflect basic kinds in reality.

A conceptualist category scheme organizes structures of cognition or representation.

Linguistic approaches examine grammatical or semantic category distinctions.

These projects can produce different taxonomies without straightforward contradiction.

== Extension ==
A formal category/class C may have an extension:

<pre>
Ext(C) = {x | x is classified under C}
</pre>

Two categories can have the same extension at one time while differing intensionally in meaning or defining criteria.

== Fuzzy membership ==
Some category systems model graded membership.

Prototype effects make items more or less typical without requiring crisp binary membership.

Formal ontology languages such as classical OWL do not natively treat ordinary class membership as graded.

== Category hierarchy semantics ==
A parent relation may mean:

<pre>
subclass-of
broader-concept-than
topic-parent
part-of
administrative-grouping
</pre>

Only the first supports ordinary subclass inference.

Systems should encode these separately when reasoning matters.

